commit 0df9bb51c	a test-only setting (sift.test_direct_max) that shortens a regression test from 41.5 s to 0.7 s; no code path of a release build changes
commit 4c1f89e29	a test-only setting of the table load that lets the tests of the ring and the workers use smaller units; the time is the test run, not a statement
commit ad95c2c1	the read of the tombstone log by page, measured by bench/churn/heapfree_churn_reads.py (the count of one tenant under track_changes, recent 20% updated)
figure over a key of several columns costs fewer instructions	bench case setup of lateral_probe_direct at 50,000 rows (a lookup table of 300,000 rows; the same data in a database under the default collation and one under the ICU collation en-US, seed 0.42): EXPLAIN ANALYZE and user instructions by perf stat of one backend, scan on, the library before and after the change, two runs each
figure over a uuid, a date, a timestamp or a timestamptz key compares it in the probe itself	bench case shape of lateral_probe_direct with a lookup table of 300,000 rows by the key type and 40,000 keys of a table of 40,000 rows (sift_col index on the driving table, the key built from its integer column): user instructions and cycles by perf stat of one backend, scan on, the library before and after the change, interleaved runs
figure a run of a correlated `LIMIT 1` child that the scan probes in its btree directly is priced by what it takes	measurement of 60 choices between the direct probes of a correlated child and the first-row table read whole (4 key kinds, tables of 30,000, 100,000 and 300,000 rows, an eightieth to a fifth of the rows as keys, lookup cache off): user instructions and cycles of one backend by perf stat, the slope over 1,250 to 20,000 keys for a probe and over the rows for a table read whole; docs/AGG.md
figure no longer counts the exit of the backend in the instructions and cycles of a statement run in a new backend	the change is to the measurement of the step bench/steps/fresh_backend_statements.sh itself (perf stat now stops before the session ends), not a claim about a query: the figures it measures are in docs/USAGE.md, from 38 statements of each arm of the step
figure planning prices the list of keys of a statement once for all the plans it makes of it (the CTE shared and inlined, and each arm that reads the list)	instructions to plan the statement of two arms over a list of keys, user instructions of one backend by perf stat, EXPLAIN of the statement run ten times after three, library before and after the change; a planning cost, not a bench case
figure compares an index tuple that has a NULL in an `INCLUDE` column or in a key column after the compared ones as fast as one that has none	measured on a scratch lab, not a bench record: the item rollup of the earlier lookup research (120,000 events with an items document, a 294,000-row lookup table with a unique expression key, a list of 200 entities, 11,388 probes), user instructions and cycles by perf stat of one backend pinned to a core, the minimum of 8 and of 14 interleaved rounds of the stock and the fixed library on two clusters of one data directory; per-function instruction counts by callgrind over the execution (nocache_index_getattr +31.7 M a statement); the regress test agg_probe_direct holds the behaviour by counts (sift._test_probe_stats slow_compares), which do not move with the host
figure `CREATE INDEX` on a table with an array column (`text`, `varchar`, `int2`, `int4` or `int8`) whose arrays repeat keeps the elements beside the whole arrays	measured on a scratch lab, not a bench record: 300,000 events with an int[] column of 441 different arrays (60% of the rows with an array), the default index against the same index with paths = 'auto:' (the earlier choice) and against a table whose index leaves the column out (the normal plan, GIN twin), user instructions and cycles of one backend pinned to a core by perf stat, the minimum of 8 interleaved rounds; milliseconds and blocks from EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS), cold after a cluster restart; leaf bytes from sift.col_leaves; the regress test col_array_auto holds the choice and the rows of the normal plan, which do not move with the host
figure options of one `sift_table`, as functions, because a table access method has no reloptions before PostgreSQL 20	instructions of one backend by perf stat (pinned CPU, minimum of ten interleaved rounds) of an INSERT of one row and of a grouped read of 20,000 rows, a build before and after the change, in a database with no option, with options on the table and with 1,000 tables with options: no bench case, since the claim is that the cost did not change; the figures are a cost of the change and not a gain, reproduced by test/t/tbl_options_cluster.sh and the regress test tbl_options for what the options do
figure a test of the elements of an array column (`@>`, `&&`, `<@`, `= ANY`) that the index holds as whole arrays only	cycles of one backend pinned to a core (perf stat), minimum of six interleaved rounds, a table of 300,000 rows with 441 distinct pairs of integers in 60% of the rows, the scan forced with sift.agg_force against the normal plan with a GIN index; no bench case: the figures justify a cost constant (instructions a row) and the choice they decide is held by the regress test agg_array_image_native
the documents that searches walk in the open write buffer of a BM25	needs the maintenance worker to act on the reads of a hundred searches of one buffer, a state the single-session case harness cannot hold still; test bm25_wbuf_read_promote asserts the rule (walked, due) and the lab numbers are in program-scale/bm25lab (mix.sh, records)
figure the documents that searches walk in the open write buffer of a BM25	lab figures of one 220,000-document index with a 5,000-document buffer (min of 3 fresh backends, cycles and instructions of the count of a term of 5%, perf stat) and the cost of one promotion; the rule is asserted by test bm25_wbuf_read_promote, records in program-scale/bm25lab
figure the write buffer of a BM25 `sift` index is opened in buffer format 6	lab figures of one 220,000-document index with a buffer of 2,000 and 5,000 documents (min of 3 fresh backends, cycles and instructions, perf stat) before and after; the rule is asserted by test bm25_wbuf_wide_bloom, records in program-scale/bm25lab
the write buffer of a BM25 `sift` index is opened in buffer format 6	a buffer of a given size in a state the case harness does not hold (a read mix over a buffer of 2,000 documents); test bm25_wbuf_wide_bloom asserts the pages skipped and the rows, lab figures in program-scale/bm25lab
figure the table of a lookup join that has its own bounds	measurement of a lookup table of 200,000 rows read for `id <= 2000` by the first statement of a fresh backend (lookup caches off, user cycles by perf stat of one backend, scan on, the library before and after the change, a grouped statement over 300,000 events with 50,000 and 200,000 groups): the load alone from EXPLAIN ANALYZE; the warm statements of bench case agg_lookup_range_bounds hold the table in the cache and do not show it
figure Fixed: pg_sift: a `sift_col` index with `track_changes` whose log is not complete only b	the figures are the cost of a one-off repair (a pass over the heap, 71 to 120 ms for 600,000 rows) and the time the worker needs after one read, measured by hand and by test/t/col_track_revouch.sh, not the speed of a case; the repair is held by test/sql/col_track_rebaseline.sql and the TAP col_track_revouch
figure the planner chooses the aggregate scan more often for a grouped aggregate of one table	the figures are the elapsed milliseconds of statements of the corpus (scripts/corpus.sh, test/corpus/lab.sql at 20,000 and 75,000 events: c2_coalesce_measures, c3_wide_expr_sums, c3_range_not_lower_inc, c3_span_length_max), of a table of 150,000 rows with the dates of a span and of test/sql/agg_key_entries_cost.sql, normal plan then scan (sift.agg_force), min of 5 EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs, parallel workers off and on; the change is the unit of the cost estimate (test/plan/bands.tsv pins the ratios, test/corpus/plans.tsv the choices), not a bench case
figure the aggregate scan prices an expression that PostgreSQL evaluates	the figures are the elapsed milliseconds of a table of 150,000 rows with 10, 11,700, 27,700, 75,100 and 150,000 distinct pairs of two integer columns (max(ends - starts) grouped by 50 tenants, forced scan, parallel workers off, min of 6 runs: 3.1, 4.3, 5.4, 8.2, 13.1 ms) and the scan estimates before and after (sift.explain_why scan_cost); the change is the unit of the cost estimate (test/sql/agg_direct_combo_cost.sql pins the ratios, test/corpus/plans.tsv the choices), not a bench case
figure the planner chooses the aggregate scan more often for a grouped aggregate with lookup joins	the figures are the user instructions, cycles, milliseconds and buffers of one statement of the plan-choice gate (scripts/plangate.sh --rows 250000 --table --lane tuned, shape linkleft_all_parents, minimum of 3 runs, normal plan then scan; the other figures are the plangate columns native_ms and scan_ms of the shapes dimright_dim_c and linkjoin_all_parents): a plan-choice measurement, not a bench case; test/plan/bands.tsv holds the cost ratios
figure a `HAVING` condition `column IN (SELECT ..)` or `EXISTS (SELECT ..)` of a plain `GROUP BY` is always answered	the 40 cells are the statements `GROUP BY gK HAVING gK IN (SELECT id FROM lst WHERE id <= L)` over 300,000 rows (K = 100 to 200,000 groups, L keeping 1% or 50% of the keys, the group columns in a sift_col index, parallel workers off and on, lookup caches off, work_mem 256MB) with the rows form taken or refused in the planner hook (cycles of the backend, minimum of 3 runs, planning included); the rule and its exactness are pinned by test/sql/agg_having_where.sql, the first figure (342 to 5.7 M instructions) by bench case agg_having_where
